el alito wrote that the  state's interest in  election integrity justify the measures  democrats like florida congressman  darren soto say  congress needs to pass new reforms the  right to vote is under attack by state  legislatures  by the supreme court and we need to pass  h.r 1 the for the people act  or the very least passed the john lewis  voting rights act but the measures are  unlikely to pass  in the narrowly divided senate in  washington  alexandra limon our washington d.c  correspondent alexander limon joining us  now to explain  why the justices ruled that way alex  what are some of the larger  repercussions that this ruling could  have  hey there well the conservative supreme  court justices  agreed with the groups that were  challenging california's law saying that  forcing  charities and non-profits to disclose  the names of their top donors  could have a chilling effect and and  prevent those donors  from making their donations and the  justices actually said that  california's requirement was infringing  on the first amendment rights of those  donors  however on the flip side of that coin  it's important to note that both  conservative groups like the americans  for prosperity foundation as well as  liberal groups like the aclu  were opposed to california's law and  and the reason for that is that it  doesn't just necessarily  impact straightforward groups that you  would typically consider  you know a charity like the american  cancer foundation  it it impacts all sorts of non-profits  that sometimes  uh you know may fundraise for certain  political causes  maybe even sometimes for political  candidates and so  other groups who were supportive of  california's law and political observers  worry  that this supreme court ruling may now  make campaign  finance laws or campaigns less  transparent  and actually open the door for a lot  more lawsuits to come forward in the  future  more directly you know trying to fight  campaign finance laws and challenge  those laws  because of this very ruling and alex in  another major case the supreme court  upheld two  controversial voting laws in arizona  what made those laws so controversial  well one of the reasons they were so  controversial and this ruling is a bit  controversial is that actually  the lower federal court that last ruled  on this case found that those  arizona voting laws disproportionately  impacted black hispanic and native  american voters  and were in violation of the 1965 voting  rights  act and those laws in arizona they did  two things  one is they allowed the state to throw  out any ballots that were cast  in the wrong precinct but secondly it  also  banned what the state of arizona calls  ballot harvesting that's going to do a  couple of things for starters  states around the country conservative  states could choose to take both of  those exact same laws and  implement them in their states because  they know that they can  no longer be challenged in court that's  already been settled but it again also  opens the door  and emboldens those uh republican or  conservative legislators around the  country that we've seen  are currently trying to pass hundreds of  different laws to  restrict voting in various ways it  emboldens them to try to move forward  even more so with those  laws with the hopes that the supreme  court in the end would uphold those laws  too  well tomorrow is america's our  independence day so we want to wish  all of you a happy fourth of july of  course now there are a bunch of  celebrations around nevada there's the  summerlin patriotic parade that was  earlier today  the boulder city danbury is also  tomorrow and after a year and a half of  being  locked in at home everybody ready to  celebrate there are fireworks shows  happening up and down the strip  downtown las vegas henderson summerlin  and the rural corners of clark county  plenty of places to celebrate our  nation's independence  we thank you very much for watching  politics now we'll see you next week  take care of yourselves  [Music]
 

